<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhutia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhutia are a people of Tibetan origin in Sikkim and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, whose ancestors migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in medieval times. They speak the Sikkimese language and follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. From 1642 Bhutia nobles and lamas formed the ruling class of the Namgyal kingdom of Sikkim, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lepcha">Lepcha</a> and later the Nepali communities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhutia.jpg" alt="Bhutia" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhutia_in_the_1860s_detail,_from-_Portret_van_een_onbekende_Bhutia_man_uit_Tibet_Bhotia._Buddhist._Tibet_(titel_op_object),_RP-F-2001-7-1122A-55_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
